<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Travancore-Cochin</h1><p class="st-meta">1949 CE – 1956 CE · capital Thiruvananthapuram</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The union of the princely states of Travancore and Cochin, an Indian state from 1949 to 1956 that became the core of Kerala.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 1 July 1949 the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/travancore">Travancore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a> were merged into the United State of Travancore and Cochin, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thiruvananthapuram">Thiruvananthapuram</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Travancore, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chithira-thirunal-balarama-varma">Chithira Thirunal Balarama Varma</a>, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajpramukh">Rajpramukh</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Travancore-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a> became a Part B state in 1950. Its politics were lively and unstable, with Congress ministries, coalitions and a strong Communist movement. The state had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India's</a> highest literacy rate and a large Christian population, and its economy rested on coconut, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and coir.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/states-reorganisation-act-1956">States Reorganisation Act</a> of 1956 the Tamil-speaking southern taluks went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madras-state">Madras State</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar</a> was added from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, forming the new state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> on 1 November 1956.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/travancore-cochin.jpg" alt="Travancore-Cochin" width="320" height="387"><figcaption>Image: Superbenjamin this file: Furfur · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Travancore-Cochin_in_India_(1951).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1949 CE – 1956 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Thiruvananthapuram</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Chithira Thirunal Balarama Varma</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism; Christianity; Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malayalam</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>United State of Travancore and Cochin, Thiru-Kochi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1952&amp;focus=travancore-cochin" aria-label="Travancore-Cochin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6806 1861 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Travancore-Cochin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7126 2117L7018 1386M7126 2117L7126 2117M7126 2117L7105 2062"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7018 1386h0M7126 2117h0M7105 2062h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7126 2117h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7126 2117h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Thiruvananthapuram. <a href="/atlas?year=1952&amp;focus=travancore-cochin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
